# Provider-neutral adapter wall

- Provider SDKs live only behind runtime adapters (`runtime/*_adapter*`, `runtime/openai_*`, etc.). No SDK imports in `core`, `tools`, `policies`, `persistence`, `observability`, or API/control-plane loading concrete adapter types—use registry/factory.
- OpenAI Agents SDK is an adapter, not orchestration core.
- Execution mode: **capability + policy**, never provider-name switches. Deterministic tool runtime for state-changing, high-risk, or policy-flagged work; provider fast path only when both gates allow low-risk work.
- Layers stay separate: runtime, orchestration, routing, tools, policies, observability. Thin controllers; no shortcuts around policy middleware. Typed I/O at tool boundaries; composition over inheritance; DI-friendly modules.
- State-changing paths: validation, policy checks, structured logs.

## Contracts

- **Runtime:** `start_session`, `run_turn`, `submit_tool_results`, `get_capabilities`, `healthcheck`.
- **Tool effects:** model intent → deterministic runtime executes side effects.
- **Policy:** pre, post, and output checks on every side-effect path.

## Checks

- No SDK imports outside adapter modules; removing one adapter must not break core imports.
- Same workflow contract runnable on at least two adapters.
- Risky tool calls blocked or rerouted when deterministic mode is required.
